CLAIMS=[
('$500 a month at ~10% a year: year 1 $6,283 (growth $283); year 3 $20,891; year 5 $38,719; year 7 ~$60,475; year 10 $102,422.','Her balance is approximately $6,283.',C+'Monthly compounding at 10%/12 with end-of-month deposits reproduces every figure exactly: 6,283; 20,891; 38,719; 60,475; 102,422. Year 5 growth $8,719 ("less than $9,000") reproduces.',[]),
('Year 15 $207,235; year 20 $379,684; year 25 ~$663,417; year 30 $1.13M; year 35 $1.9M; year 40 $3.16M, about 13x contributions of $240,000.','At year 15, Casey\'s balance hits $207,235.',C+'Same convention reproduces: 207,235; 379,684; 663,417; 1,130,244; 1,898,319; 3,162,040. 3,162,040 / 240,000 = 13.2x. Year 20 growth $259,684 = 68% of balance ("more than 2/3"; 2.16 dollars per dollar contributed).',[]),
('Daily growth: ~$10/day at year 5, ~$28 at year 10, ~$104 at year 20 (~$3,000/month), ~$866 at year 40.','That\'s about $10 a day in interest by year five.',C+'Balance x 10% / 365: $10.6, $28.1, $104.0, $866.3; all reproduce. $104 x 30 = $3,120 per month.',[]),
('Year 15 alone grows ~$22,000 from compounding (over 3x the contribution); year 25 annual growth almost $70,000, a median American salary.','her account grows by roughly $22,000',C+'Growth within year 15 (balance change minus $6,000) is $19,331, not ~$22,000 (still 3.2x the contribution). Year 25 growth is $62,571 (10% of the year-25 balance is $66,342), so "almost $70,000" overstates. '+R+'Real median personal earnings are well below that; the "median salary" comparison depends on household vs individual measures.',[]),
('First $100,000 takes ~10 years, the second ~5, the third ~3.','The second $100,000 takes her roughly five years.',C+'$100K at 9.9 years, $200K at 14.75 (4.8 years), $300K at 18.0 (3.25 years). Reproduces.',[]),
('The S&P 500 has averaged roughly 10% a year since 1926.','which is what the S&P 500 has done on average since 1926',R+'Nominal total return including dividends ~10% annualised since 1926 (Ibbotson/SBBI-type data) is consistent by recall; real return ~7%. The scenario is nominal and ignores fees and taxes. Not fetched.',[]),
('Dalbar: over 20 years the average investor earned 4.3% a year while the S&P 500 returned 8.2%; on average that cost half their potential wealth.','the average investor earned only 4.3% a year',R+'Dalbar QAIB reports of this shape (average equity fund investor ~4-5% vs S&P ~8-9% over 20 years) are consistent by recall; the exact vintage is unstated and not fetched. QAIB methodology (flow-based) is disputed. Over 20 years 1.043^20 / 1.082^20 = 0.48, so "half" is arithmetically consistent. '+U,[]),
('Jake cashes out ~$30,000 at year 4; the decision cost him $3 million; restarting $300/month at 45 he has ~$14,000 at 48 and ~$48,000 at 63.','His balance is about $14,000.',C+'Year-4 balance $29,361 ("around $30,000") reproduces; Casey minus Jake at 63 ~$3.1M, so "$3 million" is consistent as an opportunity cost. $300/month for 3 years = $12,535 (~$14,000 loose). But 18 years of $300/month at 10% gives ~$180,000 at 63, not ~$48,000; the script\'s own assumptions contradict that figure. The year-4 lump left invested alone would be ~$1.06M.',[]),
('Fidelity: the average 401(k) in someone\'s 50s is ~$212,000, median ~$77,000; Casey (year 25) is ahead of ~85% of Americans her age.','the average 401k balance for someone in their 50s is around $212,000',SN+'Search results attribute recent Fidelity 50-54 averages of ~$200,000 (Q4 2025) and medians ~$60,000-65,000; no source for $212,000 / $77,000 was located, and Fidelity\'s primary age table was not opened. Casey at year 25 is 48, not in her 50s. The 85% figure has no stated source. '+U,['FID50S']),
('Charlie Munger said the first $100,000 is a grind; roughly 99% of Buffett\'s net worth came after age 50.','Roughly 99% of Buffett\'s net worth was built after he turned 50.',R+'The Munger quote is widely attributed and is given as a paraphrase. The 99%-after-50 statistic is commonly cited (e.g. Morgan Housel); exact share depends on date. Not fetched.',[]),
('Starting 10 years later (age 33) gives ~$1.13M at 63, so waiting costs ~$2M for $60,000 of missed contributions; a dollar at 22 grows 45x over 40 years; a dollar at 42 doubles "maybe twice".','It would be about $1.13 million.',C+'30-year balance $1,130,244; 3,162,040 - 1,130,244 = $2.03M; missed contributions 120 x 500 = $60,000. 1.1^40 = 45.3x reproduces. 1.1^20 = 6.7x, about 2.75 doublings, so "maybe twice" understates.',[]),
('90% of people quit in the early levels.','you\'ll do what 90% of people do',R+'No source given; rhetorical. '+U,[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "Your Life at Every Level of Compound Interest" uses the channel\'s recurring "every level" ladder format. The script delivers seven named levels with year-by-year balances for one fictional saver and a quitter counter-example. STRONG against the 10-peer cohort (2.03x) but TYPICAL on the 5-peer window (1.83x), so the tier is unstable and sits just over the threshold. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background split into three panels headed "YEAR 1", "YEAR 10", "YEAR 30" in heavy black type. The recurring cartoon man (grey T-shirt) holds a few coins (year 1), stands beside one cash stack (year 10), and is shocked, surrounded by cash piles (year 30). At 320x180 the three headings and the growing piles stay legible. The progression matches the script\'s level structure; no numbers shown.'
SCRIPT='A seven-level compound-interest narrative: 11 p.m. brokerage-app hook; premise ($500/month at 10%, Casey vs Jake); level 1 (year 1, $283 growth); level 2 discouragement (years 2-5), Jake cashes out, Dalbar gap; level 3 first crossover ($100K, Munger); level 4 snowball (year 15); level 5 acceleration (year 20, $104/day); level 6 inflection (year 25, Fidelity 50s comparison, Jake restarts); level 7 exponential (years 25-40, $3.16M, Buffett after 50); cost of waiting 10 years; reframe ("stay in your seat"); close. All balances reproduce under monthly compounding at 10%/12. Overstated: year-15 growth ($19,331 not ~$22,000), year-25 growth ($62,571 not ~$70,000). Inconsistent: Jake\'s $48,000 at 63 contradicts $300/month for 18 years (~$180,000). Fidelity $212,000/$77,000 not located. Nominal returns, no fees or taxes. The stored ending is complete.'
